1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,788
<font color="#FFFFFF">Schrödinger Bridges connect two probability
distributions with a diffusion process,</font>

2
00:00:05,868 --> 00:00:10,767
<font color="#FFFFFF">but nearly every existing solver is heavy: it
stacks several neural networks,</font>

3
00:00:10,847 --> 00:00:14,906
<font color="#FFFFFF">needs adversarial min-max training, and can take
hours on a GPU.</font>

4
00:00:14,986 --> 00:00:19,498
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper introduces LightSB, a lightweight
Schrödinger Bridge solver.</font>

5
00:00:19,578 --> 00:00:26,935
<font color="#FFFFFF">By parameterizing the Schrödinger potential as a
Gaussian mixture and treating its logarithm as an energy function,</font>

6
00:00:27,015 --> 00:00:29,651
<font color="#FFFFFF">LightSB turns the problem into one simple,</font>

7
00:00:29,731 --> 00:00:33,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">non-minimax optimization objective with closed-form
components.</font>

8
00:00:33,805 --> 00:00:38,964
<font color="#FFFFFF">The result solves Schrödinger Bridges in moderate
dimensions in minutes on a CPU,</font>

9
00:00:39,044 --> 00:00:41,421
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no painful hyperparameter tuning,</font>

10
00:00:41,501 --> 00:00:45,560
<font color="#FFFFFF">and is provably a universal approximator of
Schrödinger Bridges.</font>

11
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:51,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">The Schrödinger Bridge problem asks for the
diffusion process between two given distributions</font>

12
00:00:51,959 --> 00:00:55,891
<font color="#FFFFFF">that stays as close as possible to a reference
Wiener process.</font>

13
00:00:55,971 --> 00:00:59,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">It is the dynamic version of entropic optimal transport,</font>

14
00:00:59,595 --> 00:01:04,433
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it underpins applications from single-cell
biology to image translation.</font>

15
00:01:04,513 --> 00:01:08,769
<font color="#FFFFFF">The trouble is that almost all existing solvers are
heavy-weighted.</font>

16
00:01:08,849 --> 00:01:14,335
<font color="#FFFFFF">They parameterize the solution with several large
neural networks and require complex,</font>

17
00:01:14,415 --> 00:01:16,341
<font color="#FFFFFF">often adversarial optimization.</font>

18
00:01:16,421 --> 00:01:20,159
<font color="#FFFFFF">As a result, the field lacks a simple, principled
baseline,</font>

19
00:01:20,239 --> 00:01:26,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">the kind of go-to method that k-means is for
clustering or Sinkhorn is for discrete optimal transport.</font>

20
00:01:27,140 --> 00:01:33,404
<font color="#FFFFFF">Consider a researcher who simply wants to compute
an entropic optimal transport or Schrödinger</font>

21
00:01:33,484 --> 00:01:36,711
<font color="#FFFFFF">Bridge between two moderate-dimensional datasets.</font>

22
00:01:36,791 --> 00:01:41,975
<font color="#FFFFFF">Today, that means adopting a solver with iterative
proportional fitting steps,</font>

23
00:01:42,055 --> 00:01:43,595
<font color="#FFFFFF">or min-max optimization,</font>

24
00:01:43,675 --> 00:01:48,184
<font color="#FFFFFF">or simulation of the full stochastic process at
every training step.</font>

25
00:01:48,264 --> 00:01:53,178
<font color="#FFFFFF">These solvers demand careful neural network design,
run for hours on GPUs,</font>

26
00:01:53,258 --> 00:01:56,012
<font color="#FFFFFF">and are sensitive to many hyperparameters.</font>

27
00:01:56,092 --> 00:02:01,411
<font color="#FFFFFF">Yet in many real settings, the user only cares
about where the trajectories end,</font>

28
00:02:01,491 --> 00:02:03,436
<font color="#FFFFFF">that is, the conditional plan.</font>

29
00:02:03,516 --> 00:02:06,743
<font color="#FFFFFF">This mismatch, heavy machinery for a modest goal,</font>

30
00:02:06,823 --> 00:02:09,780
<font color="#FFFFFF">is exactly the gap LightSB is built to close.</font>

31
00:02:10,160 --> 00:02:13,812
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

32
00:02:13,892 --> 00:02:17,543
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, it introduces LightSB,</font>

33
00:02:17,623 --> 00:02:21,275
<font color="#FFFFFF">a lightweight solver that combines two recent
ideas: parameterizing the Schrödinger potential with sum-exp quadratic,</font>

34
00:02:21,355 --> 00:02:25,007
<font color="#FFFFFF">that is Gaussian mixture, functions,</font>

35
00:02:25,087 --> 00:02:28,738
<font color="#FFFFFF">and viewing the log-potential as an energy function.</font>

36
00:02:28,818 --> 00:02:32,470
<font color="#FFFFFF">Together these yield a single, non-minimax,</font>

37
00:02:32,550 --> 00:02:36,202
<font color="#FFFFFF">simulation-free optimization objective with
closed-form expressions for the plan and the drift.</font>

38
00:02:36,282 --> 00:02:39,933
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

39
00:02:40,013 --> 00:02:43,665
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors prove that this Gaussian-mixture solver
is a universal approximator of Schrödinger Bridges,</font>

40
00:02:43,745 --> 00:02:47,397
<font color="#FFFFFF">which they note is the first ever such result.</font>

41
00:02:47,477 --> 00:02:51,128
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third,</font>

42
00:02:51,208 --> 00:02:54,860
<font color="#FFFFFF">they analyze the generalization error and show it
converges at the standard parametric rate as sample size grows.</font>

43
00:02:55,240 --> 00:02:59,427
<font color="#FFFFFF">LightSB starts from the goal of minimizing the
Kullback-Leibler divergence between the</font>

44
00:02:59,507 --> 00:03:03,695
<font color="#FFFFFF">true entropic optimal transport plan and a
parameterized plan.</font>

45
00:03:03,775 --> 00:03:07,962
<font color="#FFFFFF">The obstacle is that we do not know the true plan.</font>

46
00:03:08,042 --> 00:03:12,229
<font color="#FFFFFF">The key move is to exploit the known form of that
plan and parameterize only the adjusted Schrödinger potential.</font>

47
00:03:12,309 --> 00:03:16,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors choose an unnormalized Gaussian mixture
for this potential.</font>

48
00:03:16,577 --> 00:03:20,764
<font color="#FFFFFF">Under this choice,</font>

49
00:03:20,844 --> 00:03:25,031
<font color="#FFFFFF">two things become tractable in closed form: the
conditional distributions of the plan,</font>

50
00:03:25,111 --> 00:03:29,299
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the normalization constant.</font>

51
00:03:29,379 --> 00:03:33,566
<font color="#FFFFFF">Proposition 3.1 rewrites the KL objective as a
simple difference of two expectations,</font>

52
00:03:33,646 --> 00:03:37,833
<font color="#FFFFFF">of the log normalization under the source and the
log potential under the target,</font>

53
00:03:37,913 --> 00:03:42,101
<font color="#FFFFFF">which can be estimated by Monte Carlo and optimized
with ordinary stochastic gradient descent.</font>

54
00:03:42,181 --> 00:03:46,368
<font color="#FFFFFF">No min-max, no iterative proportional fitting,</font>

55
00:03:46,448 --> 00:03:50,635
<font color="#FFFFFF">and no simulation of trajectories during training.</font>

56
00:03:50,715 --> 00:03:54,903
<font color="#FFFFFF">Once trained, the same potential gives a
closed-form drift,</font>

57
00:03:54,983 --> 00:03:59,170
<font color="#FFFFFF">so the full bridge process can be reconstructed directly.</font>

58
00:03:59,550 --> 00:04:03,679
<font color="#FFFFFF">The solver is tested across four kinds of data.</font>

59
00:04:03,759 --> 00:04:07,888
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, a two-dimensional Gaussian to Swiss-roll toy,</font>

60
00:04:07,968 --> 00:04:12,097
<font color="#FFFFFF">which visualizes how the noise level epsilon shapes
the trajectories.</font>

61
00:04:12,177 --> 00:04:16,306
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second,</font>

62
00:04:16,386 --> 00:04:20,515
<font color="#FFFFFF">a recent high-dimensional entropic optimal
transport benchmark with known ground-truth plans,</font>

63
00:04:20,595 --> 00:04:24,725
<font color="#FFFFFF">spanning dimensions from two to one hundred
twenty-eight and several values of epsilon.</font>

64
00:04:24,805 --> 00:04:28,934
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, the MSCI single-cell dataset from a Kaggle
competition,</font>

65
00:04:29,014 --> 00:04:33,143
<font color="#FFFFFF">with cells from four human donors at four time
points, projected by PCA to fifty,</font>

66
00:04:33,223 --> 00:04:37,352
<font color="#FFFFFF">one hundred, and one thousand dimensions.</font>

67
00:04:37,432 --> 00:04:41,561
<font color="#FFFFFF">And fourth, unpaired image translation on FFHQ faces,</font>

68
00:04:41,641 --> 00:04:45,770
<font color="#FFFFFF">performed in the five-hundred-twelve-dimensional
latent space of a pretrained ALAE autoencoder.</font>

69
00:04:46,150 --> 00:04:48,846
<font color="#FFFFFF">The results show both accuracy and speed.</font>

70
00:04:48,926 --> 00:04:54,060
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the entropic optimal transport benchmark, where
the ground truth is known,</font>

71
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:59,613
<font color="#FFFFFF">LightSB reduces the conditional Bures-Wasserstein
error to well under one percent,</font>

72
00:04:59,693 --> 00:05:01,644
<font color="#FFFFFF">often around a few hundredths,</font>

73
00:05:01,724 --> 00:05:06,723
<font color="#FFFFFF">while the best previous solver sits between about
one and eighteen percent.</font>

74
00:05:06,803 --> 00:05:08,686
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the MSCI single-cell task,</font>

75
00:05:08,766 --> 00:05:13,223
<font color="#FFFFFF">LightSB reaches energy distance comparable to
strong GPU baselines,</font>

76
00:05:13,303 --> 00:05:18,505
<font color="#FFFFFF">but it trains in roughly one to two and a half
minutes on just four CPU cores,</font>

77
00:05:18,585 --> 00:05:24,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">whereas competing continuous solvers need tens of
minutes to over an hour on a V100 GPU.</font>

78
00:05:24,543 --> 00:05:25,682
<font color="#FFFFFF">And on FFHQ faces,</font>

79
00:05:25,762 --> 00:05:33,672
<font color="#FFFFFF">it performs realistic male-to-female and
child-to-adult translation in a five-hundred-twelve-dimensional latent space,</font>

80
00:05:33,752 --> 00:05:36,110
<font color="#FFFFFF">converging in under a minute on CPU.</font>

81
00:05:36,490 --> 00:05:40,636
<font color="#FFFFFF">To understand the role of the noise parameter epsilon,</font>

82
00:05:40,716 --> 00:05:44,862
<font color="#FFFFFF">the authors map a two-dimensional Gaussian to a
Swiss roll while sweeping epsilon across three values.</font>

83
00:05:44,942 --> 00:05:49,088
<font color="#FFFFFF">When epsilon is small,</font>

84
00:05:49,168 --> 00:05:53,314
<font color="#FFFFFF">the learned process is nearly deterministic and its
trajectories are almost straight lines.</font>

85
00:05:53,394 --> 00:05:57,540
<font color="#FFFFFF">As epsilon grows,</font>

86
00:05:57,620 --> 00:06:01,766
<font color="#FFFFFF">the trajectories become more volatile and the
conditional distributions at the endpoint spread out.</font>

87
00:06:01,846 --> 00:06:05,992
<font color="#FFFFFF">This matches the theory: epsilon controls the
stochasticity of the bridge.</font>

88
00:06:06,072 --> 00:06:10,218
<font color="#FFFFFF">The benchmark results also reveal that LightSB
gains the most when the target distributions</font>

89
00:06:10,298 --> 00:06:14,444
<font color="#FFFFFF">align with its Gaussian-mixture inductive bias,</font>

90
00:06:14,524 --> 00:06:18,670
<font color="#FFFFFF">while remaining accurate across the tested
dimensions and noise levels.</font>

91
00:06:19,050 --> 00:06:21,126
<font color="#FFFFFF">A few numbers capture the impact.</font>

92
00:06:21,206 --> 00:06:27,331
<font color="#FFFFFF">LightSB converges in under one minute on four CPU
cores for the five-hundred-twelve-dimensional</font>

93
00:06:27,411 --> 00:06:28,834
<font color="#FFFFFF">image translation task,</font>

94
00:06:28,914 --> 00:06:30,075
<font color="#FFFFFF">with no GPU at all.</font>

95
00:06:30,155 --> 00:06:33,994
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the hardest single-cell setting, one thousand
dimensions,</font>

96
00:06:34,074 --> 00:06:40,069
<font color="#FFFFFF">it reaches an energy distance of one point two
seven in one hundred forty-six seconds on CPU,</font>

97
00:06:40,149 --> 00:06:44,707
<font color="#FFFFFF">matching a minimax GPU solver that needs
seventy-one minutes on a V100.</font>

98
00:06:44,787 --> 00:06:47,059
<font color="#FFFFFF">On the entropic transport benchmark,</font>

99
00:06:47,139 --> 00:06:51,893
<font color="#FFFFFF">its conditional error drops to as little as three
hundredths of a percent,</font>

100
00:06:51,973 --> 00:06:56,596
<font color="#FFFFFF">against best-baseline errors ranging from about one
to eighteen percent.</font>

101
00:06:56,676 --> 00:07:03,847
<font color="#FFFFFF">And all of this comes with a theoretical guarantee:
LightSB is a universal approximator of Schrödinger Bridges,</font>

102
00:07:03,927 --> 00:07:08,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">with generalization error that vanishes at the
standard parametric rate.</font>

103
00:07:08,930 --> 00:07:14,226
<font color="#FFFFFF">The lasting message is that a Schrödinger Bridge
solver does not have to be heavy.</font>

104
00:07:14,306 --> 00:07:19,996
<font color="#FFFFFF">By parameterizing the transport potential as a
Gaussian mixture and optimizing a single,</font>

105
00:07:20,076 --> 00:07:21,701
<font color="#FFFFFF">straightforward objective,</font>

106
00:07:21,781 --> 00:07:27,339
<font color="#FFFFFF">LightSB solves entropic optimal transport and
Schrödinger Bridges in minutes on a CPU,</font>

107
00:07:27,419 --> 00:07:30,683
<font color="#FFFFFF">without adversarial training or process simulation.</font>

108
00:07:30,763 --> 00:07:34,420
<font color="#FFFFFF">It matches or outperforms much heavier GPU-based solvers,</font>

109
00:07:34,500 --> 00:07:38,813
<font color="#FFFFFF">comes with a universal-approximation guarantee, and
is easy to use.</font>

110
00:07:38,893 --> 00:07:42,026
<font color="#FFFFFF">In short, LightSB is positioned to be the simple,</font>

111
00:07:42,106 --> 00:07:46,550
<font color="#FFFFFF">reliable baseline that the Schrödinger Bridge field
has been missing.</font>
